  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "id": "mechanism_1",
      "name": "solid-solution strengthening",
      "category": "strengthening",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "solution treated and forced air cooled",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 2,
          "section": "Introduction",
          "quote": "Similarly, the yield strength of metastable β Ti alloys is also affected by four common behaviors, including element solid solution, precipitation particle, dislocation action and boundary barrier",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "4e6c27df1a3b59b2:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_2",
      "name": "dislocation pinning by solute atoms",
      "category": "strengthening",
      "sample_id": "sample_2",
      "material_state": "tensile tested at room temperature",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 1,
          "section": "Abstract",
          "quote": "The pinning between dislocations and Si atoms as well as other interstitial atoms at and near grain boundaries is mainly responsible for the high yield strength.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "4e6c27df1a3b59b2:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_3",
      "name": "dislocation slip",
      "category": "deformation",
      "sample_id": "sample_2",
      "material_state": "tensile tested at room temperature",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 1,
          "section": "Abstract",
          "quote": "Subsequently, dislocations originating from grain boundaries can easily slip in a planar pattern along the {110}〈111〉 slip systems, resulting in a continuous stress drop.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "4e6c27df1a3b59b2:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_4",
      "name": "local strain softening",
      "category": "softening",
      "sample_id": "sample_2",
      "material_state": "tensile tested at room temperature",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 5,
          "section": "Results",
          "quote": "Therefore, it can be inferred that the behavior describing flow stress drop (Fig. 2a) and work hardening rate (Fig. 2b) below 0 can be attributed to the local strain softening associated with a local increase in strain rate based on the macroscopic strain.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "4e6c27df1a3b59b2:part:001"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
